    "result_text": "I implemented `serve_http` in `app/server.py`. The tests pass, but I haven't seen the HTTP server run: my attempt to start it and send a request was blocked.\n\n```python\ndef serve_http(host: str, port: int) -> None:\n    build_server().run(\"streamable-http\", host=host, port=port)\n```\n\nThe installed MCP SDK (`mcp==2.2.0`, already a dependency) has Streamable HTTP built in, so no new packages were needed. `run(\"streamable-http\", ...)` serves the endpoint at `/mcp` by default on the host and port you pass, and blocks until the process is stopped. The stdio mode hasn't changed.\n\n**Tests:** `pytest -q` passes (2 tests). Neither test touches the HTTP mode.\n\n**To check it yourself:** run `python -m app.server --http --port 8765`, then POST an MCP `initialize` request to `http://127.0.0.1:8765/mcp`.\n\n**If remote agents can't connect:** the server listens on `127.0.0.1` by default, so only the same machine can reach it. Start it with `--host 0.0.0.0` so other machines can connect. When the host is a localhost address, the SDK also turns on DNS-rebinding protection. That protection only accepts requests whose `Host` header is a localhost name. So if agents come through a proxy or tunnel that forwards to `127.0.0.1`, their requests may be rejected. Binding to `0.0.0.0` avoids that check."
